course: reflection
lesson: 18
title: The fight I lost and the bullying I can't escape
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Losing a fight in front of people hurts the pride much more than the body: Connor got pinned down by Liam behind the bike sheds while kids chanted, and on the bus home his head ran hot: "I got battered in front of the whole year. I'm weak. I should've hit him harder. Next time I'll get him."
explain: That night he was fuming and planning revenge; for a week every laugh in the corridor felt like it was about him; after two weeks the revenge plan looked stupid and he was mostly just embarrassed; a month later he and Liam nodded at each other in the corridor.
explain: What he slowly understood: the crowd that chanted forgets fast, revenge would only start round two, and being strong is not the same as winning a fight; his older cousin told him, "Real hard men walk away," and a boxing club, where fighting has rules, helped more than the night-time plans.
explain: Bullying is different from one fight: it is being hurt on purpose again and again by someone with more power, in the corridor, in a group chat or even at home, and it lasts until a grown-up who can stop it finds out.
explain: Bullies rely on silence, and the mean words often stick and start to feel true; they are not true, and telling a trusted adult is the strongest move, not snitching.
fact: Losing a fight usually hurts pride more than the body, and the crowd that watched tends to forget within days.
fact: Revenge usually starts another round of fighting rather than ending it; walking away is often the stronger choice.
fact: "Got battered" is UK slang for got beaten up; a "scrap" is a fight; "beef" is a quarrel; to "squash it" is to end a quarrel.
fact: Bullying is hurting someone on purpose, again and again, when the bully has more power; one fight between equals is not the same thing.
fact: Cruel names about someone's body are bullying called body shaming; the words can stick and feel true, but they say more about the bullies than about the person.
fact: Bullies often rely on their target staying silent; telling a trusted adult is the best way to stop it, and schools must keep pupils safe.
fact: Telling a grown-up about bullying is not snitching or grassing; it is keeping someone safe.
fact: Cyberbullying is bullying online; taking screenshots, blocking and reporting, and showing a grown-up can help stop it.
fact: Hitting back, even when someone else started it, makes you part of the trouble; "two wrongs don't make a right".
fact: Bullies often do not see how much they hurt someone; seeing it clearly can change them, and making amends helps.
fact: Best friends can fall out over small things; saying sorry first often ends it faster than waiting.
fact: An accent shows where you or your family come from; teasing someone for it is unkind, and many classes have lots of accents.
fact: Bullying can happen at home too, from brothers, sisters or their friends; it is not "just teasing" if it scares you, and a parent needs to know.
fact: Standing up for someone is brave; it can make you a target, so telling a grown-up and sticking together with friends is safest.
exercise: Connor lost a fight in front of the whole year. What hurt most? | His pride, more than his body | His legs, more than his feelings | His homework, which got muddy | Nothing, he forgot about it at once
exercise: Two weeks later, how did Connor see his revenge plan? | It felt stupid; mostly he was just embarrassed | It felt brilliant, so he went ahead and carried it out the next day | He had forgotten about Liam completely | He wanted revenge on the whole class too
exercise: Why is revenge a bad plan after a fight? | It usually starts round two instead of ending it | It always makes the crowd like you more and forget the first fight | It is only bad if you lose the second time | It makes the first fight count for nothing
exercise: What did Connor's cousin mean by "Real hard men walk away"? | Walking away takes real strength | Tough people always run away when scared | Hard men never get into fights at all | Walking is better exercise than fighting
exercise: Zara is called cruel names about her body every day. What is true? | The words are bullying, and they are not true about her | If lots of people keep saying something, it must be true about you | It is fine because the girls are only joking | She should skip meals so the names will stop
exercise: What helped Zara? | Telling her teacher and Mum, and a club where she felt good | Skipping lunch so the girls had nothing to say | Agreeing with the girls so they would get bored | Changing schools quietly without ever telling anyone the reason why
exercise: Leo is shoved every day and thinks, "If I tell, he'll get me worse." Why should he still tell? | Bullies rely on silence, and grown-ups can stop it | The bully will always get bored and say sorry by himself in the end | Telling makes the bully stronger every time | He should only tell if he gets badly hurt
exercise: Ava was left out of a group chat and mean memes were posted about her. What is the best step? | Screenshot, block, and show a grown-up | Read the memes again and again to get used to them | Post meaner memes back about the whole group | Delete her phone so nobody can see her online
exercise: Finn punched a boy who called him names, and got in trouble too. Why? | Hitting back makes you part of the trouble | Teachers always punish whoever is smaller in a fight | The name-calling was fine, so only Finn did wrong | Finn should have hit him harder to end it quickly
exercise: Grace was the bully, until she saw the quiet girl's face. What happened next? | She felt ashamed, said sorry and sat with her at lunch | She decided the girl deserved it, shrugged, and just carried on as before | She blamed the girl for crying so easily in class | She found a new girl to make fun of instead
exercise: Sam and his best mate fell out over a football. What worked? | Saying, "Sorry, mate, can we be friends again?" | Waiting for weeks for the other one to come and say sorry to him first | Buying a new ball and keeping it for himself | Telling everyone his mate was a cheat
exercise: Kofi is teased for his accent. What is true about accents? | They show where you come from and are part of you | Some accents are wrong and need to be fixed so people can understand | Only grown-ups are allowed to have accents | Accents mean someone is worse at English
exercise: Ruby's brother and his friends call her names when parents are out. What should she do? | Tell Dad; bullying at home needs a grown-up too | Hide in her room every evening until she is old enough to leave home | Accept it, it is only teasing at home | Call them names back until they stop
exercise: Theo stood up for a bullied kid and now he is a target. What helps most? | Telling a teacher and sticking together with friends | Fighting the bullies on his own after school to show he isn't scared | Joining the bullies so they leave him alone | Pretending he never stood up for anyone
exercise: What makes something bullying rather than one fight? | It's repeated, on purpose, by someone with more power | It happens only once between two equal friends | It happens only when a teacher is watching | It only counts if somebody gets a bruise
test: Connor's mate goes, "you got proper battered, bro, gotta run it back tomorrow." ("Run it back" means do it again.) What's the better idea? | Leave it; revenge just starts round two | Do it again tomorrow with more mates | Practise punching all night in his room | Tell everyone Liam cheated and then fight
test: A month after the fight, Connor and Liam nod at each other in the corridor. What does this show? | Feelings after a fight often cool down over weeks | They are secretly planning another big fight for after the holidays | Connor has lost all his pride for good | Nodding means they will now be best friends
test: Zara's nan from Newcastle says, "Howay pet, divvn't listen to them." ("Howay" means come on; "divvn't" means don't; "pet" is a kind word for a child.) What is the nan telling her? | The bullies' words aren't true, so don't believe them | She should listen carefully to what they say | She should run away from that school for good and never go back there | She should become friends with the bullies
test: What would happen if Leo kept the shoving secret for the whole year? | It would likely carry on, with nobody able to stop it | The bully would get bored of it by himself and slowly become kind | Leo's tummy aches would go away at once | The school would find out by magic
test: Ava's sister says, "they're just memes, it's not real bullying." Which is true? | Online bullying is real bullying and can be stopped | Bullying only counts if it happens in person | Memes cannot hurt anyone because they're jokes | It only counts if a teacher sees it happen
test: Finn's dad from Glasgow says, "Aye, he started it, but ye cannae just skelp him." ("Cannae" means can't; "skelp" means hit.) What's the point? | Hitting back still gets you in trouble; tell a teacher instead | Finn should have kicked him instead of hitting, because that's allowed | It was fine because he started it first | Finn should wait until after school to fight
test: In a cartoon, the bully gets one punch from the hero and becomes nice forever. What's wrong? | Hitting usually starts more trouble; grown-ups helping changes things | Nothing; one good punch from the hero always cures any bully for good | Bullies only stop if they get two punches | Real bullies never stop being mean at all
test: Grace thinks, "I didn't think it was that bad." What made her change? | Seeing how much the girl was hurt | A teacher shouting at her loudly | The quiet girl hitting her back | Getting bored of being mean
test: Kofi's classmate drawls, "Ain't nothin' wrong with how you talk, y'all." ("Y'all" means you all, from the southern US.) What's she saying? | Every accent is fine, including Kofi's | Kofi should drop his own accent and copy the way she talks instead | Only American accents are okay | Accents are something to be fixed
test: Theo's mate goes, "lowkey you should've just minded your business." ("Lowkey" means a bit, kind of.) What's the truer answer? | Standing up was brave; now he should tell a teacher too | Yes; never help anyone who is being bullied, it's none of your business | He should fight the bullies to prove he's right | He should join the bullies to stay safe
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises bullying, as distinct from a single altercation? | Repeated, intentional harm by a party possessing greater power | A single disagreement between two parties of equal standing | Any physical contact occurring upon school premises | Conduct witnessed exclusively by members of staff
exam: Identify the principal reason for which disclosure to a trusted adult is recommended in cases of bullying. | Bullying is sustained by silence, and adults possess the means to end it | Disclosure invariably renders the perpetrator more aggressive towards the pupil | Adults are obliged to retaliate on behalf of the pupil | Disclosure is required solely where an injury is sustained
exam: It is given that a pupil retaliated physically following verbal provocation. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The retaliation renders the pupil a party to the misconduct | The retaliation is wholly excused by the prior provocation | Verbal provocation carries no consequence whatsoever | Retaliation is mandated by most school regulations
exam: In respect of bullying conducted by means of electronic messages, determine which statement is correct. | It constitutes genuine bullying and may be documented and reported | It is exempt from all consideration, as it is not conducted in person | It ceases to be harmful once the messages are deleted | It is regarded as humour and requires no intervention
